{"id": "E0127", "idea": {"id": "I262", "category": "diagnostics", "priority": 1, "source": "meta", "status": "trying:lane1", "kind": "identical", "after": ["I216"], "title": "Flag farm entries, kings, folds and feeds in the debug log line so review rule counts can tally them", "hypothesis": "This review audited bot/farm.hpp and bot/feed.hpp, and rule-counts.md could not see either: a farm entry is a best-scored step into a closed pocket that farmEntryOk accepted, and feeds, fold steps and king turns all land in 'other single step (feed, fold or king) 0.2%'. Every farm idea so far (I097, I121, I171, I224, E0095's post-mortem) built its own throwaway logging copy to count farm episodes, and this review had to infer feeds from replays (a kelp or own-body death beside a longer teammate). Change: when P.debugIndicator is on, the existing ct.output_log line in bot/main.cpp also writes \"farm\" (1 when the played move is closed and farmEntryOk accepted it, else 0), \"king\" (isKing), \"fold\" (the king id when Fold.active, else -1) and \"feed\" (1 when feedDirection decided the turn). The line is off in real games, so every normal game is identical move for move; CPU p99 must not rise. After I216, which adds the deciding rule's name to the same line. Test: a debug build where a length-4 dragon beside a closed pocket holding P.farmMinPearls + 1 pearls enters it logs farm 1; boundary: the same dragon taking an open move logs farm 0. The orchestrator extends loop/rulecounts.py to tally the new fields (farm entries a game by starting length, king split-outs, feeds by round).", "history": ["E0116 kept I216, which this idea cites: raised to priority 1; check whether what it was waiting on is now there"]}, "lane": 1, "base": "7e4b60b1f9a49decf45b4e4ba0ae1831a14d6b50", "candidate": "38046fa4f720cb8534d8fe15d5c1f1e9a592be2b", "changed_files": ["bot/main.cpp", "bot/scoring.hpp", "tests/bot/test_decisions.cpp"], "session": {"parent": "bc-main-fresh", "name": "bc-E0127-15eee9ff", "ses